<Base title="Exploring Angular Templates — Interactive Tour">
  <h2>Exploring Angular's Template Layer</h2>
  <p class="subtitle">Personal thoughts on what a future template surface might look like — explicit contracts, typed surfaces, and structures that are easier for humans, tooling, and AI agents to reason about.</p>

  <p>This is a thought experiment: what if Angular's template layer were redesigned around function-based building blocks and strict shapes? The ideas here are sketched in a <a href="https://github.com/mauriziocescon/ng-next/blob/main/types/ng-types.ts">type system</a> and a <a href="https://github.com/mauriziocescon/ng-next/blob/main/types/ng-dsl-type-checking-spec.md">type-checking spec</a> — but none of this is real Angular. It's just one person thinking out loud.</p>

  <p>The exploration touches on:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Building blocks as functions</strong> — component, directive, derivation, fragment</li>
    <li><strong>TS expressions with <code>{'{}'}</code></strong> — bindings + text interpolation</li>
    <li><strong>Explicit binding prefixes</strong> — bind:, on:, model:, class:, style:, animate:, use:</li>
    <li><strong>Hostless components + lexical scoping</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Inputs lifted up</strong> — immediately available in setup and providers</li>
    <li><strong>Expose and Template Refs</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Composition via Fragments and Forwarding</strong></li>
    <li><strong>DI Enhancements with injectionToken</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <div class="annotation">
    <strong>The DSL boundary:</strong> Template markup lives inside a <code>@{'{ }'}</code> markup literal. The <code>@</code> marks where TypeScript ends and the template DSL begins. It supports Angular control flow, directives, custom bindings, and an Angular-owned <code>IntrinsicElements</code> map for native tag typing. This is not plain TS — it would need dedicated tooling and parser support.
  </div>

  <p>Use the sidebar — or the arrow keys — to walk through each idea.</p>
</Base>
